    {
      "number": 10,
      "id": "I_kwDOQr9O0c7151jd",
      "title": "Misleading CDN motivation",
      "url": "https://github.com/intarchboard/draft-iab-ip-geo-workshop-report/issues/10",
      "state": "CLOSED",
      "author": "mnot",
      "authorAssociation": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "assignees": [
        "mnot"
      ],
      "labels": [],
      "body": "> Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) and cloud providers utilize geolocation data to route end-user traffic to the \"closest\" data center or edge node. While network latency (topological distance) is the ideal metric, physical location is often used as a heuristic for initial server selection. Misalignment here results in suboptimal routing and increased latency for users.\n\nThis is pretty problematic. CDNs (and I presume cloud providers) more often use anycast routing these days. Even When they do use DNS-based routing, they use a topological map (based upon observed performance metrics like ping times), *not* geolocation data. \n",
      "createdAt": "2026-03-24T06:28:02Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-04-08T23:31:00Z",
      "closedAt": "2026-04-08T23:31:00Z",
      "comments": [
        {
          "author": "jlivingood",
          "authorAssociation": "CONTRIBUTOR",
          "body": "@mnot Seems like a very fair point. Would you mind please submitting a PR with the changes you would like? ",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-01T14:09:23Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-01T14:09:23Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "janaiyengar",
          "authorAssociation": "NONE",
          "body": "@mnot -- my understanding is that geolocation data is used for routing, despite my thinking that it shouldn't be. I don't think we should remove that text.",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-06T18:36:13Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-06T18:36:13Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "mnot",
          "authorAssociation": "CONTRIBUTOR",
          "body": "Jana - can you explain exactly what you mean? I don't dispute that CDNs and cloud providers use a 'map' of the Internet to determine how to route requests to a network-nearby server, but I don't know of any case where a GeoIP database is used in that process. Typical practice is that it's a _topological_ map, not a geographic one.",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-06T22:43:57Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-06T22:43:57Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "tfpauly",
          "authorAssociation": "COLLABORATOR",
          "body": "Please see how IP geo is used today in:\n\nhttps://www.ietf.org/slides/slides-ipgeows-paper-moving-beyond-geographic-inference-00.pdf\n\nhttps://www.ietf.org/slides/slides-ipgeows-paper-akamai-position-paper-for-iab-workshop-on-ip-address-geolocation-ip-geo-00.pdf",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-08T03:00:34Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-08T03:00:34Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "mnot",
          "authorAssociation": "CONTRIBUTOR",
          "body": "Ah, OK - I'll work with Erik to come up with some better text. My issue is that the text will lead a reader to believe that CDN routing is predominantly done thanks to IP GEO, when his paper portrays it as one (relatively minor) input of many.",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-08T03:14:28Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-08T03:14:28Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "mnot",
          "authorAssociation": "CONTRIBUTOR",
          "body": "... and also from what I understand Akamai is fairly unique in this.",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-08T03:15:05Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-08T03:15:05Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "tfpauly",
          "authorAssociation": "COLLABORATOR",
          "body": "@mnot I suggested https://github.com/intarchboard/draft-iab-ip-geo-workshop-report/pull/13\n\nAkamai definitely does this, but Apple CDN too (although that's something I'd like to see change)",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-08T03:16:38Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-08T03:16:38Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "mnot",
          "authorAssociation": "CONTRIBUTOR",
          "body": "@tfpauly that's better, see one comment there",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-08T03:19:29Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-08T03:19:29Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "tfpauly",
          "authorAssociation": "COLLABORATOR",
          "body": "@mnot did you finish your review? I don't see a comment yet ",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-08T03:22:48Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-08T03:22:48Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "mnot",
          "authorAssociation": "CONTRIBUTOR",
          "body": "Sigh - there is something very broken in gh commit commenting at the moment.\n\nLine 489, I suggest:\n\n> is sometimes used as an input to DNS-based routing systems to assist in server selection\n\n... and omitting \"to reach\" on 490",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-08T03:28:46Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-08T03:28:46Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "janaiyengar",
          "authorAssociation": "NONE",
          "body": "LGTM -- I was going to post the exact same talks that @tfpauly did. These are examples we know, and there are probably a bunch of other smaller CDN-type entities that probably do similar things.",
          "createdAt": "2026-04-08T18:47:39Z",
          "updatedAt": "2026-04-08T18:47:39Z"
        },
        {
          "author": "tfpauly",
